WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.090 --> 00:00:03.600
- The US economy is on pace
to grow six times faster

00:00:03.600 --> 00:00:05.670
than the Fed predicted at
the beginning of the year,

00:00:05.670 --> 00:00:08.040
and to completely avoid the recession

00:00:08.040 --> 00:00:10.140
that just about every
major investment bank

00:00:10.140 --> 00:00:12.046
predicted would happen.

00:00:12.046 --> 00:00:15.840
Stocks are jumping with the
NASDAQ up by more than 35%.

00:00:15.840 --> 00:00:18.690
So why does everyone seem so bumped?

00:00:18.690 --> 00:00:20.790
It's not just that the vibes are off.

00:00:20.790 --> 00:00:22.290
There are three big reasons

00:00:22.290 --> 00:00:24.810
why the US is in economic expansion,

00:00:24.810 --> 00:00:28.440
but a confidence recession
falling, real wages,

00:00:28.440 --> 00:00:31.470
declining affordability,
and a rising imbalance

00:00:31.470 --> 00:00:34.290
between Americans' debt and their savings.

00:00:34.290 --> 00:00:36.870
It really is all about the numbers.

00:00:36.870 --> 00:00:39.240
Consumer confidence is
lower than it has been

00:00:39.240 --> 00:00:42.510
93% of the time since 1978.

00:00:42.510 --> 00:00:44.940
That decline in confidence has a lot to do

00:00:44.940 --> 00:00:46.830
with what's happening to American's pay.

00:00:46.830 --> 00:00:49.830
The latest data show that
American's wages are still rising,

00:00:49.830 --> 00:00:52.350
but by some measures they're
no longer keeping pace

00:00:52.350 --> 00:00:55.440
to the inflation, meaning
real wages are falling.

00:00:55.440 --> 00:00:57.270
The latest CPI Inflation report

00:00:57.270 --> 00:01:00.120
showed real average weekly
earnings rose in November,

00:01:00.120 --> 00:01:03.423
but had fallen in October
for the third month in a row.

00:01:04.268 --> 00:01:06.900
US households earning
less than $71,000 a year

00:01:06.900 --> 00:01:08.760
have seen their disposable income decline

00:01:08.760 --> 00:01:10.830
for five straight quarters.

00:01:10.830 --> 00:01:12.840
In addition to the stagnating wages

00:01:12.840 --> 00:01:14.280
many Americans are seeing,

00:01:14.280 --> 00:01:16.680
some things are getting
much more expensive.

00:01:16.680 --> 00:01:19.140
A new study from real estate
brokerage firm, Redfin,

00:01:19.140 --> 00:01:21.000
found that the average
monthly mortgage payment

00:01:21.000 --> 00:01:24.090
in the US has more than
doubled since 2013,

00:01:24.090 --> 00:01:27.690
and has risen by more
than $1,400 since 2020.

00:01:27.690 --> 00:01:29.970
Redfin's study also found a record low

00:01:29.970 --> 00:01:32.910
15% of homes were affordable this year,

00:01:32.910 --> 00:01:36.300
a 42% decrease from 2022,

00:01:36.300 --> 00:01:38.430
which had previously set the record

00:01:38.430 --> 00:01:40.470
for lowest number of affordable homes.

00:01:40.470 --> 00:01:42.480
Redfin defines a listing as affordable

00:01:42.480 --> 00:01:44.400
if the estimated monthly mortgage payment

00:01:44.400 --> 00:01:47.940
is no more than 30% of the
local county's median income.

00:01:47.940 --> 00:01:50.490
Childcare payments have
also jumped significantly.

00:01:50.490 --> 00:01:52.740
According to internal
data from Bank of America,

00:01:52.740 --> 00:01:54.870
the average childcare
payment per household

00:01:54.870 --> 00:01:57.840
has risen by more than 30% since 2019.

00:01:57.840 --> 00:01:59.730
And buying a car has also reached

00:01:59.730 --> 00:02:01.500
record levels of unaffordability.

00:02:01.500 --> 00:02:03.060
Car shopping website, Edmunds,

00:02:03.060 --> 00:02:05.250
found that a record high
percentage of consumers

00:02:05.250 --> 00:02:06.690
who finance their vehicle

00:02:06.690 --> 00:02:09.540
were paying more than $1,000 a month.

00:02:09.540 --> 00:02:12.540
Falling affordability and
the decline in real wages

00:02:12.540 --> 00:02:15.690
have led to Americans taking
on billions more in debt

00:02:15.690 --> 00:02:17.640
while putting less in their savings.

00:02:17.640 --> 00:02:21.240
The US personal savings
rate hit 3.8% in October.

00:02:21.240 --> 00:02:23.490
That's not only below
the long-term average,

00:02:23.490 --> 00:02:24.810
it's less than half.

00:02:24.810 --> 00:02:27.780
According to government
data going back to 1959,

00:02:27.780 --> 00:02:29.760
Moody's also found that Americans earning

00:02:29.760 --> 00:02:33.330
between 21,000 and around $71,000 a year

00:02:33.330 --> 00:02:35.190
have had a negative personal savings rate

00:02:35.190 --> 00:02:36.930
for five quarters in a row.

00:02:36.930 --> 00:02:39.510
So not only have those
folks been unable to save

00:02:39.510 --> 00:02:40.740
for more than a year,

00:02:40.740 --> 00:02:43.080
most of them have been spending more money

00:02:43.080 --> 00:02:44.370
than they're bringing in.

00:02:44.370 --> 00:02:46.740
That imbalance is showing
up in debt levels.

00:02:46.740 --> 00:02:50.910
US household debt balances
increased by $228 billion

00:02:50.910 --> 00:02:53.610
in the third quarter alone
according to the New York Fed.

00:02:53.610 --> 00:02:55.260
And after credit card balances rose

00:02:55.260 --> 00:02:58.950
by a record $154 billion
in the third quarter.

00:02:58.950 --> 00:03:01.650
Americans now have more than $1 trillion

00:03:01.650 --> 00:03:04.170
of credit card debt for
the first time ever.

00:03:04.170 --> 00:03:06.990
We're also seeing a significant
increase in Americans

00:03:06.990 --> 00:03:08.490
who can't pay off their loans.

00:03:08.490 --> 00:03:11.160
Defaults on consumer loans
rose to the highest level

00:03:11.160 --> 00:03:13.110
in more than 10 years last quarter.

00:03:13.110 --> 00:03:14.250
Put all of this together,

00:03:14.250 --> 00:03:17.010
and you can see why even
with falling inflation,

00:03:17.010 --> 00:03:20.490
booming stock prices,
and a strong job market,

00:03:20.490 --> 00:03:23.240
Americans aren't feeling all
that confident these days.

